\h 2 Corinthians
\toc1 2 Corinthians
\toc2 2 Corinthians
\toc3 2CO
\mt1 2 Corinthians
\c 1
\s1 Paul Greets the Corinthians
\r (\ref Acts 18:1–11|ACT 18:1-11\ref*; \ref 1 Corinthians 1:1–3|1CO 1:1-3\ref*)
\p \v 1 Paul, an apostle of Christ Jesus by \add the\add* will of God, and Timothy \add our\add* brother,
\p To the church of God in Corinth, together with all the saints throughout Achaia:
\p \v 2 Grace and peace to you from God our Father and \add the\add* Lord Jesus Christ.
\s1 The God of All Comfort
\p \v 3 Blessed \add be\add* the God and Father of our Lord Jesus Christ, the Father of compassion and \add the\add* God of all comfort, \v 4 who comforts us in all our troubles, so that we can comfort those in any trouble with the comfort we ourselves \add have received\add* from God. \v 5 For just as the sufferings of Christ overflow to us, so also through Christ our comfort overflows.
\p \v 6 If we are afflicted, \add it is\add* for your comfort and salvation; if we are comforted, \add it is\add* for your comfort, which accomplishes in you patient endurance of the same sufferings we experience. \v 7 And our hope for you \add is\add* sure, \add because\add* we know that just as you share in \add our\add* sufferings, so also \add you will share\add* in \add our\add* comfort.
\p \v 8 We do not want you to be unaware, brothers, of the hardships we encountered in \add the province of\add* Asia.\f + \fr 1:8 \ft Literally \fqa in Asia\ft ; Asia was a Roman province in what is now western Turkey. \f* We were under a burden far beyond our ability to endure, so that we despaired even of life. \v 9 Indeed, we felt we were under the sentence of death, in order that we would not trust in ourselves, but in God, who raises the dead.
\p \v 10 \add He\add* has delivered us from such a deadly peril, and He will deliver \add us\add*. In \add Him\add* we have placed our hope that He will yet again deliver \add us\add*, \v 11 as you help us by \add your\add* prayers. Then many will give thanks on our behalf for the favor shown us in answer to \add their prayers \add*.
\s1 Paul’s Change of Plans
\p \v 12 For this is our boast: Our conscience testifies that we have conducted ourselves in the world, and especially in relation to you, in \add the\add* holiness and sincerity that \add are from\add* God — not in worldly\f + \fr 1:12 \ft Literally \fqa fleshly\f* wisdom, but in \add the\add* grace of God. \v 13 For we do not write you anything that is beyond your ability to read \add and\add* understand. And I hope that you will understand \add us\add* completely, \v 14 as you have already understood us in part, that you may boast of us just as we  \add will boast \add* of you in the day of our Lord Jesus.\f + \fr 1:14 \ft BYZ and TR \fqa the Lord Jesus\f*
\p \v 15 Confident of this, I planned to visit you first, so that you might receive a double blessing. \v 16 \add I wanted\add* to visit you on my way to Macedonia, and to return to you from Macedonia, and then \add to have\add* you help me on my way to Judea.
\p \v 17 When I planned this, did I do it carelessly? Or do I make my plans by human standards, so as \add to say\add* “Yes, yes” and also “No, no”? \v 18 But \add as surely as\add* God \add is\add* faithful, our message to you is not “Yes” and “No.” \v 19 For the Son of God, Jesus Christ, who was proclaimed among you by me and Silvanus\f + \fr 1:19 \ft That is, Silas\f* and Timothy, was not “Yes” and “No,” but in Him it has always been “Yes.” \v 20 For all \add the\add* promises of God \add are\add* “Yes” in \add Christ\add*. And so through Him, \add our\add* “Amen” is spoken to \add the\add* glory of God.
\p \v 21 Now \add it is\add* God who establishes \add both\add* us and you in Christ. He anointed us, \v 22 placed His seal on us, and put \add His\add* Spirit in our hearts as a pledge of what is to come. \v 23 I call God as my witness that \add it was in order\add* to spare you \add that\add* I did not return to Corinth. \v 24 Not that we lord it over your faith, but we are fellow workers with you for \add your\add* joy, because \add it is\add* by faith \add that\add* you stand firm.
\c 2
\s1 Reaffirm Your Love
\p \v 1 \add So\add* I made up my mind not to make another painful \add visit\add* to you. \v 2 For if I grieve you, who \add is left\add* to cheer me but those \add whom\add* I have grieved? \v 3 I wrote as I did so that on my arrival I would not be grieved by \add those\add* who ought \add to make\add* me rejoice. I had confidence in all of you, that you \add would share\add* my joy. \v 4 For through many tears I wrote you out of great distress and anguish of heart, not to grieve you but to let you know how much I love you.
\p \v 5 Now if anyone has caused grief, he has not grieved me but all of you — to some degree, not to overstate \add it\add*. \v 6 The punishment \add imposed on him\add* by the majority \add is\add* sufficient for him. \v 7 So instead, you ought to forgive and comfort \add him\add*, so that he will not be overwhelmed by excessive sorrow. \v 8 Therefore I urge you to reaffirm \add your\add* love for him.
\p \v 9 \add My\add* purpose in writing \add you was\add* to see if you would stand the test \add and\add* be obedient in everything. \v 10 If you forgive anyone, I also \add forgive him\add*. And if I have forgiven anything, I have forgiven \add it\add* in \add the\add* presence of Christ for your sake, \v 11 in order that Satan should not outwit us. For we are not unaware of his schemes.
\s1 Triumph in Christ
\p \v 12 Now when I went to Troas \add to preach\add* the gospel of Christ and a door stood open for me in \add the\add* Lord, \v 13 I had no peace in my spirit, \add because\add* I did not find my brother Titus \add there\add*. \add So\add* I said goodbye to them \add and\add* went on to Macedonia.
\p \v 14 But thanks \add be\add* to God, who always leads us triumphantly as captives in Christ and through us spreads everywhere the fragrance of \add the\add* knowledge of Him. \v 15 For we are to God \add the\add* sweet aroma of Christ among those who are being saved and those who are perishing. \v 16 To \add the\add* one \add we are\add* an odor that brings death, to the other a fragrance that brings life.\f + \fr 2:16 \ft Literally \fqa To the one, indeed, an aroma from death to death; but to the other, an aroma from life to life.\f* And who \add is\add* qualified for such \add a task\add*?
\p \v 17 For we are not like so many others, who peddle the word of God \add for profit\add*. On the contrary, in Christ we speak before God with sincerity, as men sent from God.
\c 3
\s1 Ministers of a New Covenant
\p \v 1 Are we beginning to commend ourselves again? Or do we need, like some \add people\add*, letters of recommendation to you or from you? \v 2 You yourselves are our letter, inscribed on our hearts, known and read by everyone. \v 3 It is clear that you are a letter from Christ, the result of our ministry, written not with ink but with \add the\add* Spirit of \add the\add* living God, not on tablets of stone but on tablets of human hearts.
\p \v 4 Such confidence before God is ours through Christ. \v 5 Not that we are competent in ourselves to claim that anything \add comes\add* from us, but our competence \add comes\add* from God. \v 6 And He has qualified us \add as\add* ministers of a new covenant, not of \add the\add* letter but of \add the\add* Spirit; for the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life.
\s1 The Glory of the New Covenant
\r (\ref Exodus 34:10–35|EXO 34:10-35\ref*)
\p \v 7 Now if the ministry of death, which was engraved in letters on stone, came with \add such\add* glory that the Israelites could not gaze at the face of Moses because of \add its\add* fleeting glory, \v 8 will not the ministry of the Spirit \add be\add* even more glorious? \v 9 For if the ministry of condemnation was glorious, how much more glorious \add is\add* the ministry of righteousness! \v 10 Indeed, what was once glorious has no glory now in comparison to the glory \add that\add* surpasses \add it\add*. \v 11 For if what was fading away \add came\add* with glory, how much greater \add is the\add* glory of that which endures!
\p \v 12 Therefore, since we have such a hope, we are very bold. \v 13 \add We are\add* not like Moses, \add who\add* would put a veil over his face to keep the Israelites \add from\add* gazing at the end of what was fading away.
\p \v 14 But their minds were closed. For to \add this\add* day the same veil remains at the reading of the old covenant. It has not been lifted, \add because\add* only in Christ can it be removed. \v 15 And even to this day when Moses is read, a veil covers their hearts. \v 16 But whenever \add anyone\add* turns to \add the\add* Lord, the veil is taken away.
\p \v 17 Now the Lord is the Spirit, and where the Spirit of \add the\add* Lord \add is\add*, \add there is\add* freedom. \v 18 And we, \add who\add* with unveiled faces all reflect the glory of \add the\add* Lord, are being transformed into \add His\add* image with intensifying glory, which comes from \add the\add* Lord, \add who is the\add* Spirit.
\c 4
\s1 The Light of the Gospel
\p \v 1 Therefore, \add since God\add* in His mercy has given us this ministry,\f + \fr 4:1 \ft Literally \fqa Therefore, having this ministry, as we have received mercy,\f* we do not lose heart. \v 2 Instead, we have renounced secret \add and\add* shameful \add ways\add*. We do not practice deceit, nor do we distort the word of God. On the contrary, by open proclamation of the truth, we commend ourselves to every man’s conscience in the sight of God. \v 3 And even if our gospel is veiled, it is veiled to those who are perishing.
\p \v 4 The god of this age has blinded the minds of unbelievers, so they cannot see the light of the gospel of the glory of Christ, who is \add the\add* image of God. \v 5 For we do not proclaim ourselves, but Jesus Christ \add as\add* Lord, and ourselves \add as\add* your servants for Jesus’ sake. \v 6 For God, who said, “\add Let\add* light shine out of darkness,”\f + \fr 4:6 \ref Genesis 1:3|GEN 1:3\ref*\f* made His light shine in our hearts to give us \add the\add* light of the knowledge of the glory of God in \add the\add* face of Jesus Christ.\f + \fr 4:6 \ft SBL, NE, and WH \fqa in the face of Christ\f*
\s1 Treasure in Jars of Clay
\r (\ref Romans 6:1–14|ROM 6:1-14\ref*)
\p \v 7 Now we have this treasure in jars of clay to show that \add this\add* surpassingly great power is from God and not from us. \v 8 We are hard pressed on all \add sides\add*, but not crushed; perplexed, but not in despair; \v 9 persecuted, but not forsaken; struck down, but not destroyed.
\p \v 10 \add We\add* always carry around in \add our\add* body the death \add of\add* Jesus, so that the life of Jesus \add may\add* also be revealed in our body. \v 11 For we who are alive \add are\add* always consigned to death for Jesus’ sake, so that the life of Jesus \add may\add* also be revealed in our mortal body. \v 12 So then, death is at work in us, but life \add is at work\add* in you.
\p \v 13 And in keeping with what is written, “I believed, therefore I have spoken,”\f + \fr 4:13 \ref Psalm 116:10|PSA 116:10\ref* (see also LXX)\f* we who have the same spirit of faith also believe and therefore speak, \v 14 knowing that the \add One who\add* raised the Lord Jesus\f + \fr 4:14 \ft SBL \fqa who raised Jesus\f* will also raise us with Jesus and present \add us\add* with you \add in His presence \add*. \v 15 All this \add is\add* for your benefit, so that the grace that is extending to more and more \add people\add* may cause thanksgiving to overflow, to the glory of God.
\p \v 16 Therefore we do not lose heart. \add Though\add* our outer self is wasting away, yet our inner \add self\add* is being renewed day by day. \v 17 For our light \add and\add* momentary affliction is producing for us an eternal weight of glory \add that is\add* far beyond comparison. \v 18 \add So\add* we fix our eyes not on what is seen, but on what is unseen. For what is seen \add is\add* temporary, but what is unseen \add is\add* eternal.
\c 5
\s1 Our Eternal Dwelling
\r (\ref Romans 8:18–27|ROM 8:18-27\ref*)
\p \v 1 For we know that if the earthly tent we \add live in\add* is dismantled, we have a building from God, an eternal house in heaven, not built by human hands. \v 2 For in this \add tent\add* we groan, longing to be clothed with our heavenly dwelling, \v 3 because when we are clothed, we will not be found naked. \v 4 For \add while\add* we are in \add this\add* tent, we groan under our burdens, because we do not wish to be unclothed but clothed, so that \add our\add* mortality may be swallowed up by life. \v 5 And \add it is\add* God who has prepared us for this very \add purpose and\add* has given us the Spirit as a pledge of what is to come.
\p \v 6 Therefore we are always confident, although we know that while we are at home in the body, we are away from the Lord. \v 7 For we walk by faith, not by sight. \v 8 We are confident, then, and would prefer to be away from the body and at home with the Lord. \v 9 So we aspire to please Him, whether we are at home \add in this body\add* or away from it. \v 10 For we must all appear before the judgment seat of Christ, that each one may receive his due for the things done in the body, whether good or bad.
\s1 Ambassadors for Christ
\p \v 11 Therefore, since we know \add what it means\add* to fear the Lord, we try to persuade men. What we are is clear to God, and I hope it is clear to your conscience as well. \v 12 We are not commending ourselves to you again. Instead, we are giving you an occasion to be proud of us, so that you can answer those who take pride in appearances \add rather than\add* in \add the\add* heart.
\p \v 13 If we are out of our mind, \add it is\add* for God; if we are of sound mind, \add it is\add* for you. \v 14 For Christ’s love compels us, \add because\add* we are convinced that One died for all, therefore all died. \v 15 And He died for all, that those who live \add should\add* no longer live for themselves, but for Him \add who\add* died for them and was raised again.
\p \v 16 So from now on we regard no one according to \add the\add* flesh. Although we once regarded Christ \add in this way\add*, \add we do so\add* no longer. \v 17 Therefore if anyone \add is\add* in Christ, \add he is\add* a new creation.\f + \fr 5:17 \ft Or \fqa a new creature\f* The old has passed away. Behold, \add the\add* new has come!
\p \v 18 All this \add is\add* from God, who reconciled us to Himself through Christ and gave us the ministry of reconciliation: \v 19 that God was reconciling \add the\add* world to Himself in Christ, not counting \add men’s\add* trespasses against them. And He has committed to us the message of reconciliation.
\p \v 20 Therefore we are ambassadors for Christ, as though God were making His appeal through us. We implore you on behalf of Christ: Be reconciled to God. \v 21 \add God\add* made Him who knew no sin \add to be\add* sin\f + \fr 5:21 \ft Or \fqa a sin offering\f* on our behalf, so that in Him we might become \add the\add* righteousness of God.
\c 6
\s1 Paul’s Hardships and God’s Grace
\p \v 1 \add As God’s\add* fellow workers,\f + \fr 6:1 \ft Literally \fqa Now working together\f* then, we urge you not to receive God’s grace in vain. \v 2 For He says:
\b 
\q1 “In the time of favor I heard you,
\q2 and in \add the\add* day of salvation I helped you.”\f + \fr 6:2 \ref Isaiah 49:8|ISA 49:8\ref* (see also LXX)\f*
\p Behold, now \add is the\add* time of favor; now \add is the\add* day of salvation!
\p \v 3 We put no obstacle in \add anyone’s way\add*, so that no one can discredit \add our\add* ministry.
\p \v 4 Rather, as servants of God we commend ourselves in every way: in great endurance; in troubles, hardships, \add and\add* calamities; \v 5 in beatings, imprisonments, \add and\add* riots; in labor, sleepless nights, \add and\add* hunger; \v 6 in purity, knowledge, patience, \add and\add* kindness; in \add the\add* Holy Spirit \add and\add* in sincere love; \v 7 in truthful speech \add and\add* in \add the\add* power of God; with the weapons of righteousness in the right hand and in \add the\add* left; \v 8 through glory and dishonor, slander and praise; viewed as imposters, yet genuine; \v 9 unknown, yet well-known; dying, and yet we live on; punished, yet not killed; \v 10 sorrowful, yet always rejoicing; poor, yet \add making\add* many rich; having nothing, and yet possessing everything.
\p \v 11 We have spoken freely to you, Corinthians. Our hearts are open wide. \v 12 \add It is\add* not our affection, \add but\add* yours, that is restrained. \v 13 \add As\add* a fair exchange— I speak as to \add my\add* children— open wide your \add hearts\add* also.
\s1 Do Not Be Unequally Yoked
\p \v 14 \add Do\add* not be unequally yoked with unbelievers. For what partnership \add can\add* righteousness \add have with\add* wickedness? Or what fellowship \add does\add* light \add have\add* with darkness? \v 15 What harmony \add is there between\add* Christ and Belial?\f + \fr 6:15 \ft Scrivener’s TR and PT; many Greek sources \fqa Beliar\f* Or what \add does\add* a believer have in common with an unbeliever? \v 16 What agreement \add can exist\add* between the temple of God \add and\add* idols? For we are \add the\add* temple of \add the\add* living God. As God has said:
\b 
\q1 “I will dwell with them
\q2 and walk among \add them\add*,
\q1 and I will be their God,
\q2 and they will be My people.”\f + \fr 6:16 \ref Leviticus 26:12|LEV 26:12\ref*; Jeremiah 32:38; Ezekiel 37:27\f*
\b 
\q1 \v 17 “Therefore come out from among them
\q2 and be separate, says \add the\add* Lord.
\q1 Touch no unclean \add thing\add*,
\q2 and I will receive you.”\f + \fr 6:17 \ref Isaiah 52:11|ISA 52:11\ref*; see also Ezekiel 20:34, including LXX.\f*
\p \v 18 And:
\b 
\q1 “I will be a Father to you,
\q2 and you will be My sons and daughters,
\q2 says \add the\add* Lord Almighty.”\f + \fr 6:18 \ft See \ref 2 Samuel 7:14|2SA 7:14\ref*.\f*
\c 7
\s1 Paul’s Joy in the Corinthians
\p \v 1 Therefore, beloved, since we have these promises, let us cleanse ourselves from everything that defiles body and spirit, perfecting holiness in \add the\add* fear of God.
\p \v 2 Make room for us in your hearts. We have wronged no one, we have corrupted no one, we have exploited no one. \v 3 I do not say this to condemn \add you\add*. I have said before that you \add so\add* occupy our hearts that we live and die together \add with you\add*. \v 4 Great \add is\add* my confidence in you; great \add is\add* my pride in you; I am filled with encouragement; in all our troubles my joy overflows.
\p \v 5 For \add when\add* we arrived in Macedonia, our bodies had no rest, but we were pressed from every \add direction\add* — conflicts on the outside, fears within. \v 6 But God, who comforts the downcast, comforted us by the arrival of Titus, \v 7 and not only by his arrival, but also by the comfort \add he had received\add* from you. He told us about your longing, your mourning, \add and\add* your zeal for me, so that I rejoiced all the more.
\p \v 8 Even if I caused you sorrow by \add my\add* letter, I do not regret \add it\add*. Although I did regret \add it\add*— for I see that \add my\add* letter \add caused\add* you sorrow, but only for a short time— \v 9 \add yet\add* now I rejoice, not because you were made sorrowful, but because your sorrow led you to repentance. For you felt the sorrow that God had intended, and so were not harmed in \add any way\add* by us. \v 10 Godly sorrow brings repentance that leads to salvation without regret, but worldly sorrow brings death.
\p \v 11 Consider \add what\add* this godly sorrow has produced in you: what earnestness, \add what\add* eagerness to clear yourselves, \add what\add* indignation, \add what\add* alarm, \add what\add* longing, \add what\add* zeal, \add what\add* vindication! In every way you have proved yourselves to be innocent in this matter. \v 12 So even though I wrote to you, \add it was\add* not on account of the \add one who\add* did wrong \add or\add* the \add one who\add* was harmed, but rather that your earnestness on our behalf would be made clear to you in the sight of God. \v 13 On account of this, we are encouraged.
\p In addition to our own encouragement, we were even more delighted by the joy of Titus. For his spirit has been refreshed by all of you. \v 14 Indeed, I was not embarrassed by anything I had boasted to him about you. But just as everything we said to you \add was\add* true, so our boasting to Titus has proved to be true as well. \v 15 And his affection for you is even greater when he remembers that you \add were\add* all obedient \add as\add* you welcomed him with fear and trembling. \v 16 I rejoice that I can have complete confidence in you.
\c 8
\s1 Generosity Commended
\r (\ref Philippians 4:10–20|PHP 4:10-20\ref*)
\p \v 1 Now, brothers, we want you to know about the grace \add that\add* God has given the churches of Macedonia. \v 2 In \add the\add* terrible ordeal they suffered, their abundant joy and deep poverty overflowed into rich generosity. \v 3 For I testify \add that they gave\add* according to \add their\add* ability and even beyond \add it\add*. Of their own accord, \v 4 they earnestly pleaded with us for the privilege of sharing in \add this\add* service to the saints. \v 5 And not \add only did they do\add* as we expected, but they gave themselves first to the Lord and then to us, through \add the\add* will of God.
\p \v 6 So we urged Titus to help complete your \add act of\add* grace, just as he had started \add it\add*. \v 7 But just as you excel in everything — in faith, in speech, in knowledge, in complete earnestness, and in the love we \add inspired\add* in you\f + \fr 8:7 \ft Or \fqa in your love for us\f* — \add see\add* that you also excel in this grace of giving. \v 8 I am not giving a command, but I am testing the sincerity of your love through the earnestness of others.
\p \v 9 For you know the grace of our Lord Jesus Christ, that \add though\add* He was rich, yet for your sake He became poor, so that you through \add His\add* poverty might become rich. \v 10 And this is my opinion about what is helpful for you in this \add matter\add*: Last year \add you were\add* the first not only to \add give\add*, but even to have such a desire. \v 11 Now finish the work, so that you may complete it with the same eager desire, according to \add your\add* means. \v 12 For if the eagerness is there, \add the gift\add* is acceptable according to what one has, not according to \add what\add* he does not have.
\p \v 13 \add It is not our intention\add* that others may be relieved while you are burdened, but \add that there may be\add* equality. \v 14 At the present time, your surplus will meet their need, so that in turn their surplus will meet your need. This way there will be equality. \v 15 As it is written:
\b 
\q1 “He who \add gathered\add* much had no excess,
\q2 and he who \add gathered\add* little had no shortfall.”\f + \fr 8:15 \ref Exodus 16:18|EXO 16:18\ref*\f*
\s1 Titus Commended
\r (\ref Titus 1:1–4|TIT 1:1-4\ref*)
\p \v 16 But thanks \add be\add* to God, who put into the heart of Titus the same devotion \add I have\add* for you. \v 17 For not only did he welcome \add our\add* appeal, but he \add is\add* eagerly coming to you of his own volition.
\p \v 18 Along with \add Titus\add* we are sending the brother who is praised by all the churches \add for his work\add* in the gospel. \v 19 More than that, \add this brother\add* was chosen by the churches to accompany us with the gracious offering we administer\f + \fr 8:19 \ft See \ref 1 Corinthians 16:3–4|1CO 16:3-4\ref*.\f* to honor the Lord Himself and \add to show\add* our eagerness \add to help\add*.
\p \v 20 We \add want to\add* avoid any criticism of \add the way\add* we administer this generous \add gift \add*. \v 21 For we are taking great care to do what is right, not only in the eyes of \add the\add* Lord, but also in the eyes of men.
\p \v 22 And we are sending along with them our brother who has proven his earnestness \add to us\add* many times \add and\add* in many ways, and now even more \add so\add* by \add his\add* great confidence in you. \v 23 As for Titus, \add he is\add* my partner and fellow worker among you. \add As for\add* our brothers, \add they are\add* messengers\f + \fr 8:23 \ft Or \fqa apostles\f* of \add the\add* churches, \add the\add* glory of Christ. \v 24 In full view of the churches, then, show \add these men\add* the proof of your love and the reason for our boasting about you.
\c 9
\s1 God Loves a Cheerful Giver
\r (\ref 1 Corinthians 16:1–4|1CO 16:1-4\ref*)
\p \v 1 Now about the service to the saints, there is no need for me to write to you. \v 2 For I know your eagerness \add to help\add*, \add and\add* I have been boasting to \add the\add* Macedonians that since last year you in Achaia were prepared \add to give \add*. And your zeal has stirred most of them \add to do likewise \add*.
\p \v 3 But I am sending the brothers in order that our boasting about you in this matter should not prove empty, \add but\add* that you will be prepared, just as I said. \v 4 Otherwise, if \add any\add* Macedonians come with me and find you unprepared, we — to say nothing of you — would be ashamed of \add having been\add* so confident. \v 5 So I thought \add it\add* necessary to urge the brothers to visit you beforehand and make arrangements for the generous gift you had promised. This way, your \add gift\add* will be prepared generously and not begrudgingly.
\p \v 6 \add Remember\add* this: Whoever sows sparingly \add will\add* also reap sparingly, and whoever sows generously \add will\add* also reap generously. \v 7 Each one  \add should give \add* what he has decided in \add his\add* heart \add to give \add*, not out of regret or compulsion. For God loves a cheerful giver.\f + \fr 9:7 \ft See \ref Proverbs 22:8|PRO 22:8\ref*, LXX addition.\f* \v 8 And God \add is\add* able \add to make\add* all grace abound to you, so that in all things, at all times, having all that you need, you will abound in every good work. \v 9 As it is written:
\b 
\q1 “He has scattered abroad His gifts to the poor;
\q2 His righteousness endures forever.”\f + \fr 9:9 \ref Psalm 112:9|PSA 112:9\ref*\f*
\p \v 10 Now He who supplies seed to the sower and bread for food will supply and multiply your store of seed and will increase the harvest of your righteousness. \v 11 You will be enriched in every way to be generous on every \add occasion\add*, \add and\add* through us \add your generosity\add* will produce thanksgiving to God. \v 12 For this ministry of service is not only supplying the needs of the saints but is also overflowing in many expressions of thanksgiving to God.
\p \v 13 Because of the proof this ministry \add provides\add*, \add the saints\add* will glorify God for your obedient confession of the gospel of Christ, and \add for the\add* generosity of \add your\add* contribution to them and to all \add the others\add*. \v 14 And their prayers for you will express their affection for you, because of the surpassing grace God \add has given\add* you. \v 15 Thanks \add be\add* to God for His indescribable gift!
\c 10
\s1 Paul’s Apostolic Authority
\p \v 1 Now by the mildness and gentleness of Christ, I appeal to you — I, Paul, who \add am\add* humble when face to face with you, but bold when away. \v 2 I beg \add you\add* that when I come I may not need to be as bold as I expect toward \add those\add* who presume that we live according to \add the\add* flesh.
\p \v 3 For though we live in \add the\add* flesh, we do not wage war according to \add the\add* flesh. \v 4 The weapons of our warfare \add are\add* not \add the weapons \add* of the flesh. Instead, \add they have\add* divine power to demolish strongholds. \v 5 We demolish arguments and every presumption set up against the knowledge of God; and we take captive every thought to make it obedient to Christ. \v 6 And we will be ready to punish every \add act of\add* disobedience, as soon as your obedience is complete.
\p \v 7 You are looking at outward appearances. If anyone is confident that he belongs to Christ, he should remind himself that we \add belong to Christ\add* just as much as he \add does\add*. \v 8 For even if I boast somewhat excessively about the authority the Lord gave us for building \add you\add* up rather than tearing you down, I will not be ashamed.
\p \v 9 I do not want to seem to be \add trying\add* to frighten you by \add my\add* letters. \v 10 For \add some\add* say, “\add His\add* letters \add are\add* weighty and forceful, but his physical presence \add is\add* unimpressive, and \add his\add* speaking \add is\add* of no account.” \v 11 Such \add people\add* should consider that what we are in \add our\add* letters when absent, \add we will be\add* in our actions when present.
\p \v 12 We do not dare to classify or compare ourselves \add with\add* some who commend themselves. \add When\add* they measure themselves by themselves and compare themselves with themselves, they show their ignorance. \v 13 We, however, will not boast beyond our limits, but only within the field of influence that God has assigned to us — a field that reaches even to you. \v 14 We are not overstepping our \add bounds\add*, as if we had not come to you. Indeed, we were the first to reach you with the gospel of Christ.
\p \v 15 Neither do we boast beyond our limits in \add the\add* labors of others. But we hope that \add as\add* your faith increases, our area of influence among you will greatly increase \add as well \add*, \v 16 so that we can preach the gospel \add in the regions\add* beyond you. \add Then\add* we will not be boasting in the \add work\add* already done in another man’s territory.
\p \v 17 Rather, “\add Let\add* him who boasts boast in \add the\add* Lord.”\f + \fr 10:17 \ref Jeremiah 9:24|JER 9:24\ref*\f* \v 18 For it is not the \add one\add* who commends himself \add who is\add* approved, but the one whom the Lord commends.
\c 11
\s1 Paul and the False Apostles
\p \v 1 I hope you will put up with a little of my foolishness, but \add you are already doing that\add*. \v 2 I am jealous for you with a godly jealousy. For I promised you to one husband, to present \add you as\add* a pure virgin to Christ.
\p \v 3 I am afraid, however, that just as Eve was deceived by the serpent’s cunning, your minds may be led astray from \add your\add* simple and pure \add devotion\add* to Christ. \v 4 For if \add someone\add* comes \add and\add* proclaims \add a\add* Jesus other \add than the One\add* we proclaimed, or if you receive a different spirit \add than the One\add* you received, or a different gospel \add than the one\add* you accepted, you put up with it very easily.
\p \v 5 I consider myself in no way inferior to those “super-apostles.” \v 6 Although \add I am\add* not a polished speaker, I am certainly not lacking in knowledge. We have made this clear to you in every way possible.
\p \v 7 \add Was it\add* a sin \add for me\add* to humble myself in order to exalt you, because I preached the gospel of God to you free of charge? \v 8 I robbed other churches by accepting \add their\add* support in order to serve you. \v 9 And when I was with you and in need, I was not a burden \add to anyone\add*; for the brothers who came from Macedonia supplied my needs. I have refrained from being a burden to you in any way, and I will continue to do so. \v 10 \add As surely as the\add* truth of Christ is in me, this boasting of mine will not be silenced in the regions of Achaia. \v 11 Why? Because I do not love you? God knows \add I do\add*!
\p \v 12 But I will keep on doing what I am doing, in order to undercut those who want an opportunity to be regarded as our equals in the things of which they boast. \v 13 For such men \add are\add* false apostles, deceitful workers, masquerading as apostles of Christ. \v 14 And no wonder, for Satan himself masquerades as an angel of light. \v 15 \add It is\add* not surprising, then, if his servants masquerade as servants of righteousness. \add Their\add* end will correspond to their actions.
\s1 Paul’s Suffering and Service
\r (\ref Colossians 1:24–29|COL 1:24-29\ref*)
\p \v 16 I repeat: \add Let\add* no one take me for a fool. But if \add you do\add*, \add then\add* receive me as a fool, so that I too may boast a little. \v 17 In this confident boasting \add of mine\add*, I am not speaking as \add the\add* Lord \add would\add*, but as a fool. \v 18 Since many are boasting according to the flesh, I too will boast. \v 19 For you gladly put up with fools, since you are so wise. \v 20 In fact, you even put up with anyone who enslaves you \add or\add* exploits \add you or\add* takes advantage \add of you or\add* exalts himself \add or\add* strikes you in the face. \v 21 To \add my\add* shame I concede that we were too weak \add for that\add*!
\p Speaking as a fool, however, I can \add match\add* what anyone else dares \add to boast about\add*. \v 22 Are they Hebrews? So am I. Are they Israelites? So am I. Are they descendants of Abraham? So am I. \v 23 Are they servants of Christ? (I am speaking as if I were out of my mind.) I am so much more: in harder labor, in more imprisonments, in worse beatings, in frequent danger of death. \v 24 Five times I received from \add the\add* Jews \add the\add* forty \add lashes\add* minus one. \v 25 Three times I was beaten with rods, once I was stoned, three times I was shipwrecked. I spent a night and a day in the open \add sea\add*.
\p \v 26 In my frequent journeys, \add I have been\add* in danger from rivers \add and\add* from bandits, in danger from \add my\add* countrymen \add and\add* from \add the\add* Gentiles, in danger in \add the\add* city \add and\add* in \add the\add* country, in danger on \add the\add* sea \add and\add* among false brothers, \v 27 in labor and toil \add and\add* often without sleep, in hunger and thirst \add and\add* often without food, in cold and exposure.
\p \v 28 Apart from these external \add trials\add*, I \add face\add* daily the pressure \add of my\add* concern for all the churches. \v 29 Who is weak, and I am not weak? Who is led into sin, and I \add do\add* not burn with grief?
\p \v 30 If I must boast, I will boast \add of\add* the things \add that show\add* my weakness. \v 31 The God and Father of the Lord Jesus, who is forever worthy of praise,\f + \fr 11:31 \ft Or \fqa forever blessed\f* knows that I am not lying. \v 32 In Damascus, the governor under King Aretas secured the city of the Damascenes in order to arrest me. \v 33 But I was lowered in a basket through a window in the wall and escaped his grasp.
\c 12
\s1 Paul’s Revelation
\p \v 1 I must go on boasting. Although there is nothing to gain, I will go on to visions and revelations from \add the\add* Lord. \v 2 I know a man in Christ \add who\add* fourteen years ago was caught up to \add the\add* third heaven. Whether \add it was\add* in \add the\add* body or out of \add it\add* I do not know, \add but\add* God knows. \v 3 And I know that \add this\add* man— whether in \add the\add* body or out of \add it\add* I do not know, \add but\add* God knows— \v 4 was caught up to Paradise. \add The things\add* he heard \add were\add* inexpressible, things that man is not permitted to tell.
\s1 Paul’s Thorn and God’s Grace
\p \v 5 I will boast about such a man, but I will not boast about myself, except in \add my\add* weaknesses. \v 6 Even if I wanted to boast, I would not be a fool, because I would be speaking \add the\add* truth. But I refrain, \add so no one\add* will credit me with more than he sees in me or hears from me, \v 7 or because of these surpassingly great revelations.
\p So to keep me from becoming conceited,\f + \fr 12:7 \ft Some translators end the previous paragraph after verse 6, and begin verse 7 with \fqa So to keep me from becoming conceited because of these surpassingly great revelations,\f* I was given a thorn in \add my\add* flesh, a messenger of Satan, to torment me. \v 8 Three times I pleaded with the Lord to take it away from me. \v 9 But He said to me, \wj “My grace is sufficient for you, for \add My\add* power is perfected in weakness.”\wj* Therefore I will boast all the more gladly in my weaknesses, so that the power of Christ may rest on me. \v 10 That is why, for the sake of Christ, I delight in weaknesses, in insults, in hardships, in persecutions, \add in\add* difficulties. For when I am weak, then I am strong.
\s1 Paul’s Concern for the Corinthians
\p \v 11 I have become a fool, \add but\add* you drove me \add to it\add*. In fact, you should have commended me, since I am in no way inferior to those “super-apostles,” even though I am nothing. \v 12 The marks of a true apostle — signs, wonders, and miracles — were performed among you with great perseverance. \v 13 In what way were you inferior to the other churches, except that I was not a burden to you? Forgive me this wrong!
\p \v 14 See, I am ready to come to you a third time, and I will not be a burden, because I am not seeking your \add possessions\add*, but you. For children should not have to save up for \add their\add* parents, but parents for \add their\add* children. \v 15 And for the sake of your souls, I \add will\add* most gladly spend my money and \add myself\add*. If I love you more, will \add you\add* love me less?
\p \v 16 Be that as it may, I was not a burden to you; but crafty as I am, I caught you by trickery. \v 17 Did I exploit you by anyone I sent you? \v 18 I urged Titus \add to visit you\add*, and I sent \add our\add* brother \add with him\add*. \add Did\add* Titus exploit you \add in any way\add*? Did we not walk in the same Spirit \add and follow\add* in the same footsteps?\f + \fr 12:18 \ft Literally \fqa Did we not walk in the same Spirit? Not in the same footsteps?\f*
\p \v 19 Have you been thinking all along that we were making a defense to you? We speak before God in Christ, and all of \add this\add*, beloved, \add is\add* to build you up. \v 20 For I am afraid \add that\add* when I come, I may not find you as I wish, and you may not find \add me\add* as you wish. \add I fear\add* that there may be quarreling, jealousy, rage, rivalry, slander, gossip, arrogance, \add and\add* disorder. \v 21 \add I am afraid that\add* when I come again, my God will humble me before you, and I will be grieved over many who have sinned earlier and have not repented of their acts of impurity, sexual immorality, and debauchery.
\c 13
\s1 Examine Yourselves
\p \v 1 This \add is the\add* third \add time\add* I am coming to you. “Every matter must be established by \add the\add* testimony of two or three witnesses.”\f + \fr 13:1 \ref Deuteronomy 19:15|DEU 19:15\ref*\f*
\p \v 2 I already warned \add you\add* the second time I was \add with you\add*. \add So\add* now in my absence I warn those who sinned earlier and everyone else: If I return, I will not spare \add anyone\add*, \v 3 since you are demanding proof that Christ is speaking through me. \add He\add* is not weak in dealing with you but is powerful among you. \v 4 For He was indeed crucified in weakness, yet He lives by God’s power. For we are also weak in Him, yet by God’s power we will live with Him concerning you.
\p \v 5 Examine yourselves \add to see\add* whether you are in the faith; test yourselves. Do you not realize that Jesus Christ \add is\add* in you — unless you fail the test? \v 6 And I hope you will realize that we have not failed the test.
\p \v 7 Now we pray to God \add that\add* you will not do \add anything\add* wrong — not that we will appear to have stood the test, but that you will do what \add is\add* right, even if we appear to have failed. \v 8 For we cannot do anything against the truth, but only for the truth. \v 9 In fact, we rejoice when we are weak but you are strong, and our prayer is for your perfection.
\p \v 10 This is why I write these things while absent, so that when I am present I will not need to be severe in my use of the authority that the Lord gave me for building \add you\add* up, not for tearing \add you\add* down.
\s1 Benediction and Farewell
\p \v 11 Finally, brothers, rejoice! Aim for perfect harmony, encourage one another,\f + \fr 13:11 \ft Or \fqa listen to my appeal\f* \add be of\add* one mind, live in peace. And the God of love and peace will be with you.
\p \v 12 Greet one another with a holy kiss.
\p \v 13 All the saints send you greetings.
\p \v 14 The grace of the Lord Jesus Christ, and the love of God, and the fellowship of the Holy Spirit \add be\add* with all of you.\f + \fr 13:14 \ft Texts vary in verse numbering for the last three verses of this chapter. BYZ ends with \fqa Amen\ft .\f*
